---
// Project Argus, the full case study. Shareable, indexable, no modals.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const ARGUS_ARCH = `  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ ARGUS · two gates for AI agents             │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ V1 · release gate (local-first)             │
  │  repo/config/skills → 27 canonical rules    │
  │  → live probes (opt-in) → mcp-probe         │
  │  → R = (S_base × C_env) × P_conf            │
  │  → report.json / report.md → PASS | BLOCK   │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ V2 · runtime gateway                        │
  │  agent → [request policy → approval 428     │
  │  → upstream → redact/block → audit chain]   │
  │  → model · /healthz · /metrics              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────┘`;
---

<Base
  title="Project Argus case study · Aaron Chakraborty"
  description="A two-layer security platform for AI agents: a local-first release gate with 27 canonical rules, and a runtime gateway that blocks prompt injection before it reaches the model."
>
  <main class="pt-24 md:pt-14">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-24">
      <p class="label-mono reveal mb-3"><a class="link" href="/projects/">← projects</a> <span class="idx">/ case study</span></p>
      <h1 class="h-serif reveal text-4xl sm:text-5xl">Project Argus.</h1>
      <p class="reveal mt-4 font-mono text-[0.8rem] text-paper-dim">
        AI-agent security platform · 27 canonical rules · runtime gateway · 53 tests ·
        <a class="link" href="https://github.com/aaron-631/PROJECT-ARGUS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">public on github</a>
      </p>

      <div class="reveal mt-10 space-y-14 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.8] text-paper-dim">
        <section>
          <h2 class="label-mono mb-4"><span class="idx">01</span> · the problem</h2>
          <p>
            I found an XSS in a production AI chatbot by hand. That bug got fixed, but the class of
            bug didn't go anywhere: teams are shipping AI agents with tool access, file access, and
            network access, and almost nobody audits what those agents are actually allowed to do
            before they go live. The question Argus answers is the one I kept asking manually:
            <em class="text-paper">would I let this agent near production?</em>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="label-mono mb-4"><span class="idx">02</span> · the constraints</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-3">
            <li class="flex gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[0.65rem] h-px w-3 shrink-0 bg-accent"></span><span>Local-first. A security audit tool that phones home is its own vulnerability. V1 runs entirely on your machine; live probes are opt-in.</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[0.65rem] h-px w-3 shrink-0 bg-accent"></span><span>Deterministic where it matters. An LLM judge can nudge confidence, but it can never erase a canonical failure. Findings must be reproducible.</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[0.65rem] h-px w-3 shrink-0 bg-accent"></span><span>Read-only recon. Probing a live MCP server must never call its tools. Enumerate, don't execute.</span></li>
            <li class="flex gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[0.65rem] h-px w-3 shrink-0 bg-accent"></span><span>CI-gateable output: structured JSON and Markdown reports a pipeline can block a release on.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="label-mono mb-4"><span class="idx">03</span> · the architecture</h2>
          <pre class="pane-body overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-line bg-ink-2 font-mono text-[0.7rem] leading-snug sm:text-[0.78rem]" aria-label="Argus architecture diagram">{ARGUS_ARCH}</pre>
          <p class="mt-6">
            Two gates. <span class="text-paper">V1 is the release gate</span>: it audits an agent's
            repo, tools, permissions, secrets, MCP servers, and network config against 27 canonical
            rules with explicit capability contracts. AST analysis for Python, structured parsing
            for JSON/YAML/TOML, text rules for the rest, and a structured rule is never silently
            run against the wrong file type. Risk comes out as
            <span class="font-mono text-[0.85rem]">R = (S_base × C_env) × P_conf</span>, clamped to
            documented bounds, with deployment profiles making context explicit: a banking agent
            scores differently from a public FAQ bot, on purpose.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            <span class="text-paper">V2 is the runtime gateway</span>: it sits between the agent
            and its model. Prompt injection gets a 403 before the request reaches the model.
            Destructive tool calls are held. Business-impacting ones force human approval with an
            HTTP 428. Responses are inspected too: credential-looking output is stopped with a 502,
            sensitive fields are redacted, and every decision lands in a hash-chained audit log
            that tampering would break.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="label-mono mb-4"><span class="idx">04</span> · what I owned</h2>
          <p>
            All of it. The two-gate design, the rule taxonomy, the risk model and its clamping
            behaviour, the decision that mcp-probe would be read-only, the audit-chain format.
            This is the tool version of how I already work: break it by hand first, then encode
            what I learned so it catches the next one automatically.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="label-mono mb-4"><span class="idx">05</span> · failures & fixes</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-4">
            <li>
              <p class="font-mono text-[0.8rem] text-crit">rules firing on the wrong file types</p>
              <p class="mt-1">Early versions ran text rules against structured files and produced garbage findings. The fix was capability contracts: every rule declares what it can parse, and the engine refuses mismatches instead of guessing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p class="font-mono text-[0.8rem] text-crit">the LLM judge being too persuasive</p>
              <p class="mt-1">A semantic judge that can downgrade findings is a prompt-injection target. Now it can only nudge P_conf within bounds; canonical failures are immune to it by construction.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="label-mono mb-4"><span class="idx">06</span> · results</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-3 font-mono text-[0.85rem]">
            <li><span class="text-accent">▸</span> real-world run: official pinned MCP filesystem server, 14 tools enumerated, 0 tool calls made, 2 HIGH findings, verdict BLOCK</li>
            <li><span class="text-accent">▸</span> has scanned the configs of Claude Code, Codex CLI, and Gemini CLI</li>
            <li><span class="text-accent">▸</span> 53 tests + Black + Flake8 + mypy + schema checks, green on every push</li>
            <li><span class="text-accent">▸</span> reproducible JSON/Markdown reports, documented end-to-end in WORKFLOW.md</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="border-t border-dashed border-line pt-8">
          <p class="font-mono text-[0.8rem]">
            <a class="link" href="https://github.com/aaron-631/PROJECT-ARGUS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">read the source</a>
            · <a class="link" href="/projects/#argus">back to projects</a>
            · <a class="link" href="/projects/vantallm/">previous case study: VantaLLM →</a>
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
